    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "A number is written at each vertex of a triangular pyramid, but these numbers are now invisible. On each edge of the pyramid we write the sum of the two numbers at its endpoints. Three of these numbers are shown. What is the number on the edge with the question mark?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2023-g0910/q09.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "11"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "12"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "13"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "14"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "we cannot be sure"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Opposite edges",
        "body": "Two edges of a tetrahedron that don't share a vertex are called opposite; together they use all four vertices once. So for each pair of opposite edges, the two edge numbers add up to the sum $S$ of all four vertex numbers.\n\nIn the figure, the edge marked 8 is opposite the edge marked 13, so $S = 21$. The edge marked 9 is opposite the edge with the question mark, so $? = 21 - 9 = 12$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 12**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Name the vertices",
        "body": "Let the apex be $t$, the left, right and front base vertices $l, r, f$. From the figure: $t + l = 8$, $t + r = 9$, $r + f = 13$, and we want $l + f$.\n\n$l + f = (t + l) + (r + f) - (t + r) = 8 + 13 - 9 = 12$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 12**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "algebra",
