    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Triangle $ABC$ is right angled. Points $D$, $E$ and $F$ are located so that $BD = BE$ and $CE = CF$. What is the measure of the angle $\\angle DEF$?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2023-g0910/q14.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$30^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$37^\\circ 30'$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$45^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$52^\\circ 30'$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "It can vary according to the size of angle $\\angle B$ of the triangle."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Two isosceles triangles",
        "body": "The right angle is at $A$, so $\\angle B + \\angle C = 90^\\circ$.\n\nTriangle $BDE$ is isosceles with $BD = BE$, so its base angles are $\\angle BED = \\frac{180^\\circ - \\angle B}{2} = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\angle B}{2}$. Similarly, in triangle $CEF$: $\\angle CEF = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\angle C}{2}$.\n\n$E$ lies on $BC$, so\n$$\\angle DEF = 180^\\circ - \\angle BED - \\angle CEF = \\frac{\\angle B + \\angle C}{2} = 45^\\circ.$$\nIt does not depend on the shape of the triangle.\n\n**Answer: (C) 45°**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Try a special case",
        "body": "If the answer is a fixed number, check an isosceles right triangle: $\\angle B = \\angle C = 45^\\circ$. Then $\\angle BED = \\angle CEF = 67.5^\\circ$ and $\\angle DEF = 180^\\circ - 135^\\circ = 45^\\circ$. The general computation above shows it is always $45^\\circ$, so (E) is wrong.\n\n**Answer: (C) 45°**"
      }
    ],
