    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "How many three-digit positive integers $x$ are there, such that subtracting the sum of digits of $x$ from $x$ gives a three-digit number whose digits are all the same?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "2"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "3"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "20"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "30"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Factor the difference",
        "body": "Write $x = 100a + 10b + c$. Then $x - (a + b + c) = 99a + 9b = 9(11a + b)$, which does not depend on $c$.\n\nA three-digit number with equal digits is $111d = 3\\cdot 37\\cdot d$. So $9(11a+b) = 111d$, i.e. $3(11a+b) = 37d$. Hence $37 \\mid 11a + b$ and $3 \\mid d$.\n\n$11a + b$ is at most $11\\cdot 9 + 9 = 108$, so $11a + b \\in \\{37, 74\\}$:\n\n- $11a + b = 37$: $a = 3$, $b = 4$ (difference $333$).\n- $11a + b = 74$: $a = 6$, $b = 8$ (difference $666$).\n\nThe last digit $c$ is free (10 choices), so there are $2\\cdot 10 = 20$ numbers: $340, \\dots, 349$ and $680, \\dots, 689$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 20**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Check an example",
        "body": "$x = 345$: $345 - 12 = 333$ ✓. Changing the last digit changes $x$ and its digit sum by the same amount, so all of $340$–$349$ work, and similarly $680$–$689$ ($680 - 14 = 666$). The first solution shows there are no others.\n\n**Answer: (D) 20**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
