        "title": "Remainders repeat with period 3",
        "body": "Number the boxes 1 to 9. The triples in boxes $k, k+1, k+2$ and $k+1, k+2, k+3$ both have sums divisible by 3, so the numbers in boxes $k$ and $k+3$ leave the same remainder mod 3. So boxes $\\{1,4,7\\}$, $\\{2,5,8\\}$ and $\\{3,6,9\\}$ each contain numbers with one common remainder.\n\nThe numbers 1–9 contain exactly three of each remainder: $\\{3,6,9\\}$, $\\{1,4,7\\}$, $\\{2,5,8\\}$. So each group of boxes gets one remainder class. Conversely, any such filling works, because three adjacent boxes then contain the remainders 0, 1, 2, with sum divisible by 3.\n\nCount: assign the 3 remainder classes to the 3 groups of boxes ($3! = 6$ ways), then order the three numbers inside each group ($6$ ways each): $6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6 = 6^4$.\n\n**Answer: (A) $6^4$**"
